        48.09014892578125,
        148.6471405029297,
        168.43881225585938,
        161.906982421875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "This latter result nevertheless calls for some comments since ρP is—perhaps surprisingly—",
      "bbox": [
        58.052215576171875,
        163.78993225097656,
        443.60955810546875,
        175.35662841796875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "independent of N, implying the rather negative property that increasing N does not seem to",
      "bbox": [
        48.090423583984375,
        176.5819854736328,
        443.61163330078125,
        185.81732177734375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "improve convergence of the algorithm. Again the IMH nature of the PIMH sampler sheds some",
      "bbox": [
        48.090423583984375,
        188.53709411621094,
        443.6036682128906,
        197.77243041992188
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "light on this point. In simple terms, the convergence properties of an IMH sampler are governed",
      "bbox": [
        48.090423583984375,
        200.49220275878906,
        443.6056213378906,
        209.7275390625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "by the large values of the ratio of the target density to the proposal density. Indeed, leaving a",
      "bbox": [
        48.090423583984375,
        212.4473114013672,
        443.6005554199219,
        221.68264770507812
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "state with such a large ratio is difﬁcult and results in a slowly mixing Markov chain exhibiting",
      "bbox": [
        48.090423583984375,
        224.4024200439453,
        443.6076354980469,
        233.63775634765625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "a ‘sticky’ behaviour. What the second result above tells us is that the existence of such sticky",
      "bbox": [
        48.090423583984375,
        236.35752868652344,
        443.6035461425781,
        245.59286499023438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "states is not eliminated by the PIMH strategy when N increases. However, the probability of",
      "bbox": [
        48.090423583984375,
        248.31263732910156,
        443.610595703125,
        257.5479736328125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "visiting such unfavourable states can be made arbitrarily small by increasing N by virtue of the",
      "bbox": [
        48.09039306640625,
        260.26776123046875,
        443.60858154296875,
        269.5030822753906
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "results in theorem 1 and the application of Tchebychev’s inequality. In fact, as a particular case",
      "bbox": [
        48.090423583984375,
        272.2228698730469,
        443.6054992675781,
        281.45819091796875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
